  <section class="brx-sec" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Does my Wirral heating need a power flush?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The clearest sign is a radiator that is warm at the top and cold at the bottom. If you spot two of the signs below, it is worth a check.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Cold at the bottom.</strong> Sludge sinks to the base of the radiator. Bleeding will not fix it.</li>
        <li><strong>Some rooms never warm up.</strong> On microbore estate homes, part-blocked pipes starve some radiators of flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Black water when you bleed a radiator.</strong> That is iron oxide sludge, and it is going through your boiler.</li>
        <li><strong>The house takes far longer to warm up.</strong> Flow gets weaker as pipes clog.</li>
        <li><strong>A banging or kettling boiler.</strong> Water is boiling in a blocked heat exchanger. We see this a lot in older Birkenhead and Wallasey systems.</li>
        <li><strong>The boiler cuts out on cold days.</strong> It works in summer but trips in January, because it cannot shed heat fast enough.</li>
        <li><strong>Stiff or seized radiator valves.</strong> Sludge can jam valves on older estate systems in CH43, CH46 and CH62.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>When a flush will not help:</strong> it will not fix a broken pump or a failed boiler part. If your boiler keeps losing pressure, that is a leak, and it needs finding first. We check this before we suggest a flush.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="microbore">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can you power flush microbore heating on the Wirral?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. Many Wirral homes built from about 1975 to 1995 have microbore pipes. We flush them often, with a method made for the narrow pipe.</p>
      <p>Microbore pipe is 8mm or 10mm wide. Standard pipe is 15mm or 22mm. The thin pipe fills with sludge much faster. You will find it in a lot of estate homes in Moreton, Bebington, Bromborough and Eastham.</p>
      <h3>How to spot it</h3>
      <p>Look at the pipes running along your skirting boards to the radiators. Microbore is much thinner than normal copper pipe. It often runs from a central point, called a manifold.</p>
      <h3>How we flush it</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>We let the cleaner work through the system first, to break the sludge down.</li>
        <li>We flush each microbore leg on its own, not the whole circuit at once.</li>
        <li>We reverse the flow many times to shift sludge from the joins.</li>
        <li>We never rush it. A microbore flush usually takes a full day.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The wrong method can push lumps of sludge into the joins and make things worse. That is why we look at the pipes before we start. The price stays fixed by radiator count.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How long does a Wirral power flush take?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most Wirral homes take one day. We start in the morning and finish in one visit.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Home</th><th>Typical time</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Flat or small terrace in CH41 to CH45, up to 6 radiators</td><td>4 to 5 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Victorian terrace or post-war semi in CH42, CH43 or CH63</td><td>5 to 7 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Microbore estate home in Moreton, Bebington or Bromborough</td><td>7 to 9 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Large home in CH47, CH48 or CH60 to CH64, 13 radiators or more</td><td>A full day</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What happens on the day</h3>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Check and test (20 minutes).</strong> We look at the boiler, pipes and radiators. We check for microbore and weak spots. Then we test the water.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the cleaner.</strong> Fernox F3 for a standard clean, or Sentinel X400 for heavy sludge. On microbore, it runs for a while before the machine goes on.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect the machine (30 minutes).</strong> Our Kamco CF90 Quantum2 pump goes on at the heating pump or across a radiator.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush each radiator (most of the day).</strong> Every radiator is flushed on its own, with the flow reversed. This is the slow part.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush until clear.</strong> We run until the water comes back clean, then test it again.</li>
        <li><strong>Inhibitor, balance and report (30 minutes).</strong> We add inhibitor to BS 7593, balance the radiators and give you a written report.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Know how many radiators you have?</strong> Call and we will give you a fixed Wirral price on the phone.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="right">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is a power flush right for my Wirral home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If your system is over 10 years old with cold radiators or black water, usually yes. If it is newer with mild signs, a £195 chemical clean may do.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>A flush is a good fit if</h3>
          <ul class="brx-list">
            <li>You have cold spots, slow warm-up or a noisy boiler</li>
            <li>You have a sealed system with standard pipes</li>
            <li>You have a microbore estate home from 1975 to 1995</li>
            <li>You are fitting a new boiler to your old radiators</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Talk to us first if</h3>
          <ul class="brx-list">
            <li>Your boiler keeps losing pressure (that is a leak)</li>
            <li>You have an old gravity-fed system with a loft tank</li>
            <li>Your pipes and radiators are badly rusted</li>
            <li>Only one radiator is cold</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Some older homes in Birkenhead and Wallasey still run on gravity-fed heating. We flush these in a different way, with the loft tank isolated. On very rusty systems, new radiators may be the better buy. See our <a href="/radiators-wirral">Wirral radiator prices</a>, from £245 supplied and fitted.</p>
      <p>We will tell you which job you need, not sell you the bigger one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="new-boiler">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a power flush before a new boiler on the Wirral?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Usually yes, if the new boiler is going onto an old system. Boiler makers need the system cleaned and treated to BS 7593, or they can refuse a warranty claim.</p>
      <p>Old sludge can damage a new heat exchanger in months. A drain and refill does not meet the standard. Keep our written report with your boiler papers as proof.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Getting a new boiler from us?</strong> We check the system on every <a href="/boiler-installation-wirral">Wirral boiler installation</a>. If it needs a flush, it is £350 on the same job.</p></div>
      <h3>Keeping it clean after</h3>
      <p>Fit a magnetic filter to catch new sludge. It is £150 with a flush. Then have the inhibitor checked at your yearly <a href="/boiler-service-wirral">boiler service</a>. A clean system can go many years without another flush.</p>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our power flushes are done by our Gas Safe registered engineers, part of our team led by James. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk/">Gas Safe Register</a>. Every job has a 12-month workmanship guarantee and £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
